# Shell scripts keep LF endings in the working tree, on every platform.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is true on the machine this repository is developed on, so
# without this a checkout rewrites every script to CRLF. Measured: a CRLF
# shebang still EXECUTES under Git for Windows, so that is not the hazard. The
# hazard is quieter — a carriage return lands inside the quoted value of every
# assignment, which for scripts/githooks/scan-private-data.sh means a CR at the
# end of a regular expression, and the last alternative of a pattern then
# matches nothing. A private-data check that silently stops matching is the
# exact failure this repository's first invariant is about (CLAUDE.md §1: an
# allow-list's error mode must be visible).
#
# The index already stores LF, so this changes checkout only — no file content
# changes with it.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The git hooks have no extension and need naming individually.
scripts/githooks/pre-commit text eol=lf
scripts/githooks/commit-msg text eol=lf
scripts/githooks/pre-push  text eol=lf

# Files the install scripts COPY off this tree keep LF too, for a different
# reason: the installers compare the installed copy against the repository's to
# decide whether the operator has local edits worth protecting. With
# `core.autocrlf` on, a worktree checkout and the main checkout can hold the same
# file with different endings, so installing from one and then checking from the
# other reports a difference whose diff shows no visible change — and exits
# non-zero, which inside a landing sequence reads as a failed step. Pinning them
# makes every checkout produce the same bytes. Measured on 2026-08-11: exactly
# that phantom difference appeared the first time a skill was installed from a
# worktree and re-checked from `master`.
knowledge.toml       text eol=lf
plugin/skills/**/*.md text eol=lf
plugin/**/*.py        text eol=lf
plugin/commands/*.md  text eol=lf
